From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivo Nunes <ivo@nunes.uk>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 19:23:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rendering: add scenario_shift() to move a whole scenario at
 once

A floating origin keeps a large world near (0, 0, 0) by moving all of it
back under the player now and then. Moved node by node, every instance
in the scenario is updated as if it alone had moved: its bounds are
recomputed, every shadow it casts is marked dirty, its render data is
rewritten and its place in the culling trees is updated. On a city of
about 32,000 nodes that took about 22 ms of the next frame's setup.

When everything moves together nothing moves relative to anything else,
so none of that is needed. RenderingServer.scenario_shift() adds the
offset to every instance's transform and bounds, moves the culling
trees' volumes as they are (DynamicBVH::shift()), keeps omni and spot
shadows and light pairings, moves reflection probes without
recapturing them (reflection_probe_instance_shift()), and resets
geometry's motion vectors as a teleport does. Instances already queued
for a full update are left to it.

No upstream issue yet.

Test: in a window, meshes under a world shifted by (-1000, 0, 0) are
found by instances_cull_aabb() 1 km away and not where they were, and
those outside it where they stayed (Purr's samples/world). On a city of
about 32,000 nodes, a shift's renderer setup fell from about 22 ms to
1.5 ms.

